from pathlib import Path
import json,html,re
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
r=json.loads((R/'results.json').read_text());s=json.loads((R/'stress-results.json').read_text())
labels={'unfiltered':'Original combined strategy','align_benchmark':'Signal agrees with price versus strike','both_indicators':'Both indicators actively agree','ask_le_60':'Buy price at most 60¢','ask_le_70':'Buy price at most 70¢','spread_le_3':'Bid–ask spread at most 3¢','distance_ge_05':'Distance at least 0.5 volatility unit','distance_ge_1':'Distance at least 1 volatility unit'}
def table(head,rows):return '<table><thead><tr>'+''.join('<th>'+html.escape(x)+'</th>' for x in head)+'</tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+html.escape(str(x))+'</td>' for x in row)+'</tr>' for row in rows)+'</tbody></table>'
mainrows=[];splitrows=[]
for k,v in r['filters'].items():
 a=v['all'];b=v['account_25pct'];ci=a['mean_net_daily_block95']
 mainrows.append([labels[k],f"{a['n']} ({a['n']/774:.1%})",f"{a['accuracy']:.2%}",f"{100*(a['mean_price']+a['mean_fee']):.2f}¢",f"{100*a['mean_net']:+.2f}¢",f"${b['ending_cash']:,.2f}"])
 splitrows.append([labels[k],f"{v['early']['n']}: {v['early']['mean_net']*100:+.2f}¢",f"{v['late']['n']}: {v['late']['mean_net']*100:+.2f}¢",f"[{ci[0]*100:+.2f}, {ci[1]*100:+.2f}]¢"])
sizing=[]
for i,allocation in enumerate((.25,.05,.02,.01)):
 sizing.append([f'{allocation:.0%}']+[f"${r['sizing'][fam][i]['ending_cash']:,.2f}" for fam in ('stochastic','sma','mixed')])
body='''<h1>Was there a pattern that could have avoided the losses?</h1><p class="lead">One simple filter made money in this historical replay, but none is established as a reliable loss-prevention rule. Smaller positions and stopping after losses protected capital without improving the underlying predictions.</p><p>Kalshi hourly BTC contracts · September 13–October 17, 2026 UTC · $500 independent paper accounts · original decision minute15 and hypothetical entry minute16, leaving44minutes before close.</p>
<h2>The promising pattern: price already far from the strike</h2><p>Take the existing combined stochastic/SMA signal only when the absolute gap between current BTC price and the contract strike is at least one estimated volatility unit for the remaining45minutes. This kept65 of774 signals (8.4%), won57 (87.69%), and ended at<strong> $650.91</strong> after fees with25% allocation per trade. The worst settled-cash peak-to-trough drawdown was<strong>48.28%</strong>.</p>
<p>This measures distance from the benchmark, not a new crossover. At minute15, the calculation uses the last completed5m close and the standard deviation of the last288 complete simple5m returns: <code>abs(spot − strike) / (spot × rolling return std × sqrt(9)) ≥ 1</code>. Missing/zero volatility fails closed. The volatility scale is a heuristic, not a proven normal distribution or calibrated winning probability. Coinbase spot is a proxy; Kalshi settles against the final60-second BRTI average.</p>
<p>Illustration only: with an$80,000 strike and a$100 estimated volatility scale, prices at$80,100 or$79,900 meet the distance threshold. The original signal still chooses YES or NO. We did not add an agreement-with-price condition:63 of65 retained signals happened to agree with the current side of the strike.</p>
<h2>All seven filters, including failures</h2><p>Each condition was fixed before this diagnostic's outputs and applied separately. No combinations or threshold optimization were tried. These are eligible signals, not necessarily financed trades: the unfiltered account could afford only283 of774. One-contract average net includes every eligible signal and avoids depletion bias. Ending cash uses actual chronology, integer quantities, fees, and settlement availability.</p>'''
body+=table(['Rule','Signals / retained','Win rate','Mean unit cost + fee','Net per contract','Ending $500 account'],mainrows)
body+='''<p>All774 eligible quotes already had spreads at most3¢, so that filter changed nothing. Requiring both indicators to agree gave a tiny one-contract average profit but lost most of the finite account: varying prices, position quantities and compounding mean equal-unit average net does not determine account growth.</p><h2>Did the pattern persist within the sample?</h2><p>Both slices have already been inspected during prior research. Neither is an untouched holdout. Counts and fee-inclusive one-contract means below are descriptive. Daily block-bootstrap intervals use2,000 resamples, seed20261018, and are not adjusted for the seven added filters or the earlier large indicator search.</p>'''
body+=table(['Rule','Sep13–28: count / net','Sep29–Oct17: count / net','Full-sample 95% mean-net interval'],splitrows)
body+='''<p>The distance≥1 rule was positive in both slices (+1.64¢ and+2.72¢ per contract), but the full interval spans<strong>−6.23¢ to+9.06¢</strong>. Only65 signals and8 losses support the result. It is a candidate for a new frozen test, not evidence of guaranteed or established profitability. These observations also do not establish the indicators' incremental value over distance alone.</p><h2>Cost stress for the positive filter</h2><p>These checks were added after seeing its positive result; they verify sensitivity, not independent confirmation. Historical quotes assume the full quantity fills at the displayed price, without proven depth, queue position or publication latency.</p>'''
body+=table(['Fill / rounding scenario','Trades','Ending cash','Worst settled-cash drawdown'],[[a['scenario'],a['trades'],f"${a['ending_cash']:,.2f}",f"{a['max_settled_balance_drawdown_pct']:.2f}%"] for a in s['accounts']])
body+='''<h2>What would have reduced the capital destruction?</h2><p>Keep the original signals, change only the maximum debit including fees as a percentage of current settled cash. Smaller allocation reduces the damage; every original strategy still loses. Each cell is a separate account.</p>'''
body+=table(['Allocation / trade','Stochastic','SMA','Combined'],sizing)
body+='''<p>A separate combined-strategy25% account that permanently halted new entries after a10% decline from its settled-cash peak stopped after the second trade and retained<strong>$412.53</strong>. It had first risen to$549.64. A single loss then crossed the threshold, producing a24.95% peak drawdown. A10% halt is therefore not a10% guaranteed loss cap when a trade can risk25%. This is an entry freeze after settlement, not a modeled liquidation stop.</p>
<h2>Why high accuracy did not protect us</h2><p>The original combined strategy won63.70% of eligible signals, but its average one-contract price plus fees was67.27¢ per$1 payout. That produced−3.58¢ per contract. For the distance≥1 subset, observed wins were87.69% against85.45¢ mean cost including fees, giving+2.24¢. Buying cheaper contracts alone did not help: their win rates also fell. A reliable rule needs a calibrated probability advantage over executable price plus fees, not accuracy by itself.</p>
<h2>Evidence and limits</h2><p>The original three25% account ledgers were reproduced field by field. Every new replay's quantity, fee, budget and settled cash was checked with exact rational arithmetic. The full mixed prediction array was reconstructed from its two components, the decision price matched the locked baseline, and all774 source shadow observations matched. A separate previously verified engine reproduced the distance-filter result and its cost stresses.</p>
<p>This study does not trade, deploy, change live risk settings, or demonstrate live execution. It covers Kalshi because historical executable Polymarket quotes were not available in the accepted study. Further historical rule-search risks fitting noise; the candidate should be frozen and assessed on genuinely new market observations before adoption.</p>
<p>Files: <a href="results.json">complete results</a> · <a href="protocol.json">fixed protocol</a> · <a href="observations.jsonl">774 observations</a> · <a href="stress-results.json">cost stresses</a> · <a href="analyze.py">reproducible analysis</a>.</p><p>Official fee assumptions: <a href="https://kalshi.com/docs/kalshi-fee-schedule.pdf">Kalshi fee schedule</a> and <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/getting_started/fee_rounding">fee rounding</a>. Source market and fee metadata are inherited unchanged from the accepted early-entry study.</p>'''
body=''.join(part if part.startswith('<') else re.sub(r'(?<=[A-Za-z])(?=[0-9$])|(?<=%)(?=[A-Za-z])',' ',part) for part in re.split('(<[^>]*>)',body))
style='body{font:17px/1.65 system-ui,sans-serif;color:#172936;background:#f7f9fb;max-width:1200px;margin:0 auto;padding:44px 32px}h1{font-size:38px;line-height:1.2}h2{margin-top:42px;font-size:25px}.lead{font-size:22px}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;background:white;font-size:15px;margin:24px 0}th,td{text-align:left;padding:12px;border-bottom:1px solid #dde4ea}th{background:#e5edf3}a{color:#075b99}code{white-space:normal;background:#e6edf2;padding:3px}strong{color:#093b55}'
(R/'report.html').write_text('<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Hourly BTC loss patterns</title><style>'+style+'</style><main>'+body+'</main></html>')
print(R/'report.html')
